{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795302463,"nonce":"TaLjiA5DnkpDDW2wty3senyXxxQ=","message":"Step 2 is done in 15b6dbcd. Any thread that allocates can now start a collection while other threads run. The switch is ZYNTAX_IMMIX_THREADS=1 and it is off by default.\n\nHow it works.\n\nA thread joins the world at its first slow-path allocation. It leaves when it exits or when the runtime calls thread_leave. A collection takes the world lock and sets a stop flag. Then it waits until every other thread has parked or entered a blocking region.\n\nEach thread computes its own stack windows and the addresses its runtime holds. The windows include fiber stacks and leave out hidden ranges. The thread publishes both before it stops. The collector marks from all of them, sweeps, bumps an epoch and wakes the others. A thread that slept through a collection resets its open regions.\n\nIf a thread does not answer within 20 ms, the collector cancels the stop. The next attempt waits one more floor of allocation. The wait doubles on each failure, up to eight floors. An explicit collection waits one second and does not back off. The trace names the thread that did not answer.\n\nIf a thread other than the owner runs a collection, the after-collection functions wait for the owner. The owner runs them at its next refill. The function realloc registers its thread first, because it reads tables that a sweep rewrites.\n\nTests. Five new unit tests cover these cases:\n- A collection reads the stack of a thread it stopped.\n- Eight threads keep their lists through automatic collections.\n- A thread in a blocking region does not hold up a collection.\n- A thread that never answers cancels the stop and backs off.\n- Two hundred threads that start and exit during collections leave no stale record.\n\nI broke the stack publication on purpose. Three of the tests failed, and I changed the first one until it failed as well. The tests then passed 150 times in a row.\n\nReal programs, Mac release build, flag on against flag off. The 43 rows give equal output, no failure and no cancelled stop. The wall time ratio is 0.88 to 1.03 as medians of three runs. Python conformance passes 50 of 50, at the default floor and in stress mode, which collects at every refill. Lua conformance passes 46 of 46 and official_suite passes 4 of 4 at the default floor. So the runtime's own helper threads do not hold up a collection in these programs.\n\nFinding. Lua official/cstack.lua hangs at a floor of 1 MB when four runs overlap. It hangs the same way with the flag off. One collection took 14 s to mark 319 KB, because a loaded machine delays tier-up and the interpreted recursion makes the stack huge. This is the pacing weakness in 3bbb229. It is not caused by this step.\n\nNot done yet. Until step 3, a thread that blocks in zrtl_thread outside a blocking region makes every collection time out and cancel. That costs memory, not correctness. Step 4 must still keep thread start-up arguments alive. Step 5 runs the stress loops on the Mac and the NUC.","files":null}]}